<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 2.6 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-vectors-ii" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Vectors II</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      You have already worked with vectors in two dimensions: writing them as column vectors, adding them, multiplying them by scalars and finding their magnitudes. The world has three dimensions, so this chapter takes the same ideas into space.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will locate points and write column and position vectors in three dimensions, express vectors in terms of the unit vectors <m>\mathbf{i}</m>, <m>\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{k}</m>, and find the magnitude of a vector in three dimensions. You will then test whether vectors are parallel and whether points are collinear, divide a line in a given ratio, derive and use the ratio theorem, and apply vectors to prove results in geometry.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vectors in three dimensions are used to track aircraft and drones, to position satellites, and in the engineering of buildings and machines.
    </p>
  </introduction>

